+# Daily Grind WebMCP
+
+The homepage registers client-side tools through `document.modelContext.registerTool` when available, with a fallback to `navigator.modelContext` for earlier browser previews. Ordinary order buttons work without WebMCP. No server, API key, or dependency is needed.
+
+Use a secure context (HTTPS or localhost) and a browser with WebMCP enabled. Browser agents discover these tools through the browser's WebMCP integration:
+
+| Tool | Example input | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `get_seasonal_menu` | `{}` or `{"query":"maple"}` | Matching drink IDs, names, descriptions, and detail links |
+| `order_drink` | `{"drinkId":"maple-oat-latte","quantity":2}` | Adds to the visible draft order and returns its updated contents |
+| `highlight_drink` | `{"drinkId":"cold-brew"}` | Highlights, focuses, and scrolls to the drink card |
+| `get_drink_order` | `{}` | Current draft quantities and selected drink ID |
+
+Drink IDs are `maple-oat-latte`, `dark-roast-espresso`, and `cold-brew`. Menu data comes from the homepage cards. Query matching is case insensitive and searches names and descriptions; a query with no matches returns an empty list. Quantity defaults to one and must be an integer from one to ten, with at most ten of each drink in an order. Invalid inputs return structured errors without modifying the order.
+
+Orders are in-memory drafts scoped to the current tab. Refreshing resets them. The visible **Clear order** button resets the draft. No payment, fulfillment, or café submission occurs. Agent actions and manual buttons use the same functions; updates are announced through a live status region. Tool registrations use an abort signal for page lifecycle cleanup and are restored when returning from the back-forward cache.
+
+The API follows the evolving [WebMCP specification](https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webmcp/). Registration failures are reported in the developer console and do not disable the page controls.
